report **STATUS: approved**  
  view show view2 V3 Columbia coauthorship by field and subfield, 2021–2024
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── answer ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
The new hierarchical edge-bundling view keeps the same 80 researchers and 208 weighted coauthorship ties as the force-directed view, which remains available for
comparison. It groups researchers into 14 OpenAlex fields and 35 subfields. Blue curves connect researchers in different fields; gray curves stay within a      
field. Thicker curves indicate more shared works. [G1, J1, V3]                                                                                                  

94 of 208 ties (45%) cross field boundaries, including 85 of 195 (44%) in the largest connected group. Environmental Science and Medicine have the most         
cross-field ties (21). [F7, T8]                                                                                                                                 

To untangle the largest group, set Component focus → Largest component and Tie scope → Cross-field only. You can also raise Minimum shared works without moving 
the researchers. If curves overlap, select or search for a researcher to see their collaborators and shared-work counts. [V3]                                   

Field and subfield labels are inferred from the primary topics of each researcher’s Columbia-affiliated works, not from department appointments. A blue tie     
means the two researchers were assigned to different fields; it does not mean their shared work was itself interdisciplinary. [T7, F7]                          

Possible follow-ups: Which researchers connect the most fields? Which field pairs still stand out after filtering to recurring collaborations?                  
